NAME
       cat - print files, one after another

SYNOPSIS
       cat [OPTION...] [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
       cat writes each FILE to standard output in turn (the name comes from
       "concatenate"). With no FILE, or when FILE is -, it reads standard
       input. It is the quickest way to look at a short file; for long files
       use less.

OPTIONS
       -n, --number
              Number every output line.

       -b, --number-nonblank
              Number only lines that are not empty.

       -s, --squeeze-blank
              Print several empty lines in a row as one.

       -A, --show-all
              Show hidden characters: tabs as ^I, line ends as $.

       -E, --show-ends
              Show a $ at the end of each line (finds trailing spaces).

       -T, --show-tabs
              Show tabs as ^I.

EXAMPLES
       Join two files into a third:

           cat part1.txt part2.txt > whole.txt

       Write a small file from the keyboard (end with Ctrl+D):

           cat > /root/notes.txt

       Write a file in a script with a here document:

           cat > /etc/motd <<'EOF'
           Welcome to server1
           EOF

SEE ALSO
       less(1), head(1), tail(1), tac(1)
